---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import NoteIllustration from "../../../components/NoteIllustration.astro";
import NoteCover from "../../../components/NoteCover.astro";
import { SITE, notes } from "../../../data/site";

const note = notes.find((item) => item.slug === "why-expert-sites-undersell-the-expert")!;

const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: note.title,
    description: note.summary,
    datePublished: note.date,
    author: {
      "@type": "Person",
      name: SITE.principal.name,
      url: SITE.principal.url
    },
    publisher: {
      "@type": "Organization",
      name: SITE.name,
      url: SITE.url
    }
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={note.title}
  description={note.summary}
  path={`/notes/${note.slug}/`}
  image={`/images/og/notes/${note.slug}.png`}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Notes · <time datetime={note.date}>{note.dateDisplay}</time></p>
    <h1>{note.title}</h1>
    <p>{note.summary}</p>
    <div class="note-hero-art"><NoteCover slug={note.slug} /></div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-body" data-analytics-component="article_body">
    <p>
      My wife is a physician who founded and runs a healthcare company,
      wrote State Change, and can hold a room. For years her website read
      like a polite brochure. Meet her and you remember the conversation for
      a week. Visit the site and you'd forgotten it by lunch.
    </p>
    <p>
      I rebuilt it. Some of what I fixed mattered and some of it didn't, and
      the gap between those two is the actual subject here.
    </p>

    <NoteIllustration kind="gap" />

    <h2>The site opened with credentials</h2>
    <p>
      Board-certified, published in, featured on, founder of. Those answer
      "is she qualified," which is not the question anyone arrives with.
      They arrive with "does this person understand what's happening to me."
      An expert who describes your problem more precisely than you can is
      presumed competent. Credentials confirm that presumption. They can't
      create it.
    </p>

    <h2>The content was filed by format</h2>
    <p>
      A blog tab, a podcast tab, a press tab, a books tab. That's an
      archive, and an archive puts the work on the reader. Nobody wants the
      podcast. They want the answer about sleep, in whatever form it happens
      to exist. Reorganizing around questions instead of formats did three
      things at once: people found things, Google finally understood what
      the site was about, and each new piece started making the older ones
      easier to find instead of burying them.
    </p>

    <h2>The exits assumed a decision nobody had made yet</h2>
    <p>
      A newsletter box in the footer, a contact page behind a nav link. Both
      are built for someone who has already decided, and they quietly lose
      everyone else, which is nearly everyone. What works is meeting people
      at their actual temperature. Someone who just read about a problem
      they have is warm. Give them something small and adjacent, right
      there.
    </p>

    <NoteIllustration kind="small-yes" />

    <h2>Now the part I had wrong</h2>
    <p>
      I assumed the site would be the growth engine. The newsletter had a
      strong run this summer, and when I traced the spikes they lined up
      with our social lead's Instagram work, not with anything I'd built. She'd
      been repurposing high-performing posts from a few months back with
      fresh subscribe links. I had the dashboard. She had the cause. Some of
      it gave back as unsubscribes caught up, which is its own lesson about
      calling a peak a trend.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the argument for measuring in the first place. Without it I'd
      have kept crediting the site and kept investing in the wrong surface.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'd still make all three fixes. A site that undersells you is a tax
      you pay on every introduction. But the site is usually where interest
      converts, not where it originates, and it's worth knowing which one
      you're building.
    </p>
    <p>
      The through-line in all three problems is boring. The site was
      assembled out of parts, a bio here, a blog there, a form somewhere, by
      different people at different times, and nobody owned the whole thing.
      Her actual work has a spine. The site presenting it didn't. That's why
      I build the product, the content, and the measurement as one thing
      rather than three separate projects.
    </p>
    <p class="article-cta" data-analytics-component="article_cta" data-analytics-event="cta">
      If yours has that problem you probably already know. It's the thing
      you apologize for before you <a class="text-link" href="/free-teardown/" data-analytics-event="cta">send
      the link</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
